            {
                id abi-headers
                title "Decide whether the port ships histedit.h, editline/readline.h and the man page"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:51:34.552190783Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Raised by errata-reconcile, which could not adjudicate two register entries without it.\n\nERR-terminal-64 (doc/editline.3 lists SIGSTOP among the signals libedit handles, which it does not) and most of ERR-core-api-34 (annotation errors in histedit.h) both carry a \"fix the documentation\" disposition. The port ships no histedit.h and nobody has touched doc/editline.3, so neither can be resolved or honestly called reproduced. They sit at open/partial until this is settled.\n\nThe question is not cosmetic. A drop-in replacement is consumed at two layers:\n\n- Link time, which is the .so and its exported symbols. That part is decided and being built.\n- COMPILE time, which is the headers. A consumer that does `#include <histedit.h>` and builds against us needs a header from somewhere. If we do not ship one, we are drop-in only for already-compiled binaries, and any consumer rebuilding from source still needs libedit installed — which rather defeats the purpose for a distribution that wants to replace it outright.\n\nShipping headers pulls in consequences worth deciding deliberately rather than discovering:\n\n- The C's headers carry the annotation defects the register already logs. Shipping them verbatim reproduces those defects at compile time for every consumer; fixing them diverges from the artifact we are replacing. That is the same reproduce-versus-fix fork conformance-policy governs, arriving in a place the policy does not obviously reach, because a header is not runtime behaviour.\n- histedit.h declares el_set/el_get as variadic. Our ABI is fixed-arity per the varargs decision, which is correct on x86-64 SysV and aarch64 AAPCS and wrong on aarch64 Apple. A header we ship is where that would have to be stated.\n- editline/readline.h has the completion_matches const mismatch ERR-readline-53 records, which is only observable to a compiling consumer — so it is a header-only defect that shipping would either propagate or fix.\n\nNot blocking anything today. Worth settling before the register is called reconciled, since two entries are parked on it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id abi-missing-exports
                title "Export the nine symbols we are missing, eight of which deployed consumers already reach"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:57:51.013898095Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Measured by conformance/abi-shape.sh, not inferred. The port exports 198 dynamic symbols against the oracle's 227: 29 missing, ZERO extra.\n\nTwenty of the 29 are the vis family and are missing CORRECTLY. Debian's libedit.so.2 has NEEDED libbsd.so.0 and imports strvis@LIBBSD_0.0, strunvis@LIBBSD_0.0 and vis@LIBBSD_0.0 while exporting none of them; the port exports and imports none, so it matches the deployment target. The oracle exports all twenty only because libbsd-dev is absent on this host, so configure answered no to HAVE_VIS/HAVE_UNVIS and src/Makefile.am compiled vis.c and unvis.c in. That is a property of the oracle's build configuration and not a gap. abi-shape.sh already splits the missing set so these never masquerade as one. Do NOT export them — see the interposition hazard on abi-surface.\n\nThe nine that are real:\n\n    ct_decode_string\n    ct_encode_string\n    fn_complete\n    fn_complete2\n    fn_display_match_list\n    fn_filename_completion_function\n    fn_tilde_expand\n    reallocarr\n    wcsdup\n\nEIGHT of those nine — all but wcsdup — are exported by Debian's libedit.so.2 as well. So a consumer deployed today reaches them, and linking against us gives an unresolved symbol. That is the whole drop-in claim failing for that consumer, not a cosmetic gap. Only _el_fn_complete is declared in histedit.h, but export is export: the symbol table is the contract, not the header.\n\nwcsdup is its own problem and belongs with the rule correction rather than here. src/wcsdup.c:14 is `#ifndef HAVE_WCSDUP` and :16 is `#include \"config.h\"`, so the guard is evaluated before the macro exists, is always true, and the bundled wcsdup always compiles — overriding glibc's for anything that links libedit. Exporting ours therefore reproduces an override the C does by accident. Decide that deliberately rather than by symmetry; note Debian's libedit IMPORTS wcsdup@GLIBC_2.2.5 rather than exporting it, so the deployed target and the oracle already disagree.\n\nTwo version-skew data points from the same run, which is exactly why the oracle is the in-tree C: the oracle and the port both export rl_kill_full_line and Debian does not, and Debian imports wcsdup where the oracle exports it.\n\nReadline coverage needs no work: port 149, Debian's libedit 148, oracle 149, against libreadline.so.8's 736. No readline symbol Debian's libedit exports is missing from the port.\n\nRe-run conformance/abi-shape.sh after this lands; it is the acceptance test and it currently FAILs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:44:11.760030167Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Seven exported, one deliberately not, and the ninth stopped being a question.\n\nEXPORTED (7), all of them symbols Debian's libedit.so.2 exports too, in two new modules of nshedit-abi that mirror the two private headers:\n\n  crates/nshedit-abi/src/chartype.rs     ct_encode_string, ct_decode_string\n  crates/nshedit-abi/src/filecomplete.rs fn_complete, fn_complete2,\n                                         fn_display_match_list,\n                                         fn_filename_completion_function,\n                                         fn_tilde_expand\n\nThe core already had every body; this is the C face of it. Two shapes were needed. (1) ct_buffer_t is a four-word struct the caller owns, and the core's CtBufferT is Vec-shaped, so each call lifts the four words into the core's struct and lowers them back — Box<[T]> round-trips because into_boxed_slice makes capacity equal length, which is the only way to store an owning buffer in a struct with no capacity word. The blocks therefore live IN the caller's struct exactly as the C's el_realloc'ed ones do, and conv.cbuff reads back as the pointer the call returned. (2) fn_complete2's app_func and attempted_completion_function are C function pointers, and the core takes bare Rust `fn`s with nowhere to carry a captured value, so the caller's pointer is parked in a thread-local for the call and a fixed adapter reads it — with a save/restore guard so a callback that re-enters fn_complete2 does not lose its own hooks. The app_func result is interned into a leaked &'static str, once per distinct value, because the C's contract for it is \"a literal the caller must not free\".\n\nreadline.c's own forwarders now forward, as the C does: tilde_expand, filename_completion_function and rl_filename_completion_function call the exported fn_* symbols instead of duplicating them, and FILENAME_SCAN moved with the function it belongs to.\n\nNOT EXPORTED — reallocarr. Decided, not defaulted, and abi-shape.sh prints the whole argument at every run rather than carrying it as a silent expectation.\n\n  - dec:libedit:posix-only-scope names it: \"strlcpy, strlcat, getline, wcsdup and reallocarr leave scope; they are libc gap-fillers with no observable surface.\" The symbol table disproves the last clause — Debian exports it — so the decision is held on the other two.\n  - It is not an interface. No installed header declares it (src/Makefile.am:55 installs histedit.h and editline/readline.h only); its prototype is in the private src/sys.h; and it has exactly one caller in all of libedit, src/wcsdup.c:38, which on a glibc host now compiles to nothing. It is an internal helper with default visibility, not a symbol a consumer was offered.\n  - dec:libedit:no-c-ffi gives no route. reallocarr reallocates a block the CALLER allocated, so its size is unknown here and std::alloc::System cannot be handed the Layout GlobalAlloc::realloc requires; only libc's realloc/free would do. That is a fourth site on a closed enumeration, and the two-part test refuses it on the second half: no rule in the corpus specifies the behaviour, because .config/nspec/config.styx excludes src/reallocarr.c and the function has no def or sem rule at all. Fabricating a Layout for System::realloc is the \"route to a nearby question\" that decision rejects by name.\n  - Residual risk, recorded rather than hidden: a consumer that declared reallocarr itself and resolved it through libedit.so.2 gets an unresolved symbol against us, and glibc has no other provider. That is the cost of holding both decisions and it is the one remaining gap in the drop-in claim.\n\nNOT A QUESTION ANY MORE — wcsdup. src/wcsdup.c was corrected (by the user, outside this node) to include config.h ABOVE its #ifndef HAVE_WCSDUP guard, so config.h's #define HAVE_WCSDUP 1 is now visible, the bundled copy is not compiled, and the oracle's export count went 227 -> 226. The C now agrees with Debian's libedit.so.2, which imports wcsdup@GLIBC_2.2.5. Not exporting it was already right and stays right — sem:histedit.wcsdup-fn and the ELF interposition hazard on abi-surface — and the name is kept on abi-shape.sh's decided list so the reasoning survives a host where HAVE_WCSDUP is genuinely 0.\n\nSame fix landed in src/reallocarr.c, where it changes nothing: HAVE_REALLOCARR is undefined on glibc either way, so #if !HAVE_REALLOCARR was accidentally right and the file compiles in both orderings. Two files had the ordering bug; strlcpy.c, strlcat.c, vis.c and unvis.c all include config.h before their guard and are correct. The symbol table agreed with the reading in all six.\n\nMEASURED, before and after (oracle rebuilt clean after the C fix):\n\n  before   port 198, oracle 226, 21 missing (20 vis + reallocarr... plus the\n           7 ct_/fn_), 0 extra          -> conformance-abi-shape FAIL\n  after    port 205, oracle 226, missing = 20 vis + 1 decided, 0 real,\n           0 extra                       -> conformance-abi-shape PASS\n\nAll five harness stages pass. The differential is unchanged at zero divergences: 247 operations under C.UTF-8 and 246 under C, identical.\n\nabi-shape.sh now splits MISSING three ways — vis family, decided, everything else — fails on the third and on an EXTRA exactly as before, and additionally fails if a name reaches the decided list with no recorded reason. The list is two literal names; it cannot absorb a new gap."
                    }

