            {
                id abi-file-surface
                title "Argue and build the FILE * surface, which blocks H_SAVE_FP, EL_SETFP and foreign streams"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:09.978302682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "This is one wall behind four separate symptoms, which is why it is its own node rather than a footnote on each.\n\nMeasured by the first conformance run: H_SAVE_FP and H_NSAVE_FP return -1 with \"can't write history\" and write ZERO bytes, where the oracle writes 322 under C.UTF-8 and 306 under C. Four of the six divergences in a 247-operation trace are this. The other two are abi-narrow.\n\nThe same wall also blocks:\n- EL_SETFP, which has no escape hatch at all.\n- el_init with caller-supplied FILE * streams; the ABI crate currently assumes fds 0/1/2, so an application handing us its own streams gets the wrong descriptors.\n- rl_initialize's tcgetattr ECHO test, which is the one divergence in the whole register that fails OPEN — we edit on non-echoing input where the C would not.\n\nCause: CFile is an opaque FILE * and dec:libedit:no-c-ffi bars fputs, ftell and fileno through it. nshedit::history::history_save_fp (crates/nshedit/src/history.rs:1608-1625) documents the hole deliberately, and el.rs:442-458 has `fn fileno(_stream: CFile) -> i32` as a stub.\n\nTHE DECISION COMES FIRST, and no-c-ffi says where. Its enumeration is closed and names this surface as a CANDIDATE third site, not an approved one: \"It passes the test on its face, since a caller's FILE * is opaque to Rust and sem:el.el-init-fn is specified in terms of fileno(fin), but dec:libedit:platform-layer defers it and the enumeration above stays closed until it is argued here.\" So amend no-c-ffi or find a route that does not need the exception — do not just add the extern and move on.\n\nThe two-part test any argument has to carry, from that decision: no pure-Rust route delivers the facility, AND a rule in the corpus specifies behaviour that cannot be implemented without it. Both halves. Note the current failure mode is defensible — \"can't write history\" is an error the C can itself produce — so the honest framing is that we return a plausible error instead of doing the work, which a differential exposes and a unit test would not have.\n\nWorth checking before assuming the exception is needed: whether the caller's FILE * can be reached through its file descriptor alone once fileno exists, since std::fs::File::from_raw_fd would then carry the write without naming any other libc symbol. That would make this one symbol rather than a family, and buffering interleaving between the C's FILE and our writes becomes the question to answer instead.\n\nAcceptance: the four H_SAVE_FP / H_NSAVE_FP lines in conformance/differential.sh stop diverging."
                    }

